  <% if @revealed_token.present? %>
    <%# Keeps this one render out of Turbo Drive's snapshot cache, which is what makes the panel's
        "only time this token is shown" claim below actually true. Without it Turbo snapshots the
        live DOM — plaintext token and all — when the reader navigates away, and repaints it on Back
        or on any return to this page; `connect()` fires on a restored snapshot exactly as on a
        fresh render, so auto-copy would also re-run and overwrite a clipboard the reader has since
        put something else on.

        It has to be this meta and not `data-turbo-cache="false"` on the panel: the element-level
        attribute is NOT implemented in turbo-rails 2.0.23 — `PageSnapshot#clone` only resets
        selects, blanks password inputs and drops `<noscript>`, and the sole cache opt-out Turbo
        reads is `getSetting("cache-control")`, i.e. this tag. Turbo evaluates it against the live
        head in `Visit#loadResponse`, which caches the outgoing snapshot before `mergeHead` runs.

        Scoped to the render that carries a token: it is emitted only inside this branch, and an
        untracked meta is provisional, so Turbo removes it on the way out and the rest of the app
        keeps its snapshot cache. %>
    <% content_for :head do %>
      <meta name="turbo-cache-control" content="no-cache">
    <% end %>
    <% revealed_heading = @revealed_token_regenerated ? "Your regenerated API key" : "Your new API key" %>
    <%# The panel is given an id and a scroll controller because a correct render is not enough here.
        This template is very tall and the control that produced this panel — mint, or rotate — sits
        near the bottom of it, while this renders near the top. Turbo Drive preserves scroll across
        the visit that follows the redirect, so the reveal can be perfect and arrive off-screen, and
        the person who pressed the button sees nothing happen. There is no second chance: only a
        digest is stored, so a missed reveal costs a rotation, which breaks whatever still carries
        the old token.

        Two mechanisms, deliberately, because they fail in different places. `ApiKeysController`
        redirects to `#revealed-key`, which a browser honours with no JavaScript at all and which
        Turbo honours on an ordinary advance visit; `scroll_into_view_controller.js` covers the case
        where Turbo restores the previous scroll position instead of consulting the fragment.
        Neither names a pixel, so neither rots as this page keeps growing.

        The second is not redundancy, and that is measured rather than assumed. The hypothesised
        cause does not reproduce in headless Chromium — Turbo scrolls to the top there, and the
        reveal is the first panel on the page, so it lands by coincidence. Driving
        `spec/system/api_key_reveal_spec.rb` against a page that had been given a scroll-restoring
        listener: with neither mechanism, both examples fail; with the fragment ALONE, both still
        fail; with the controller, both pass. The fragment earns its place for the no-JavaScript
        reader, and the controller is what actually closes the reported failure.

        The id is on the PANEL rather than on an inner element so the whole thing — heading,
        warning, token and prompt — comes into view together. %>
    <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(
          title: @revealed_token_name.present? ? "#{revealed_heading}: #{@revealed_token_name}" : revealed_heading,
          id: "revealed-key",
          data: { controller: "scroll-into-view" }
        ) do %>
      <%# Auto-copy is declared here rather than defaulted on in the controller because it is right
          for exactly this call site: overwriting a clipboard is cheap and reversible, and leaving
          this page without the token is neither. The two outcome messages are passed in rather
          than hardcoded in JS, so the words a reader sees live with the rest of the words on this
          page. %>
      <div class="space-y-3"
           data-controller="copy-text"
           data-copy-text-auto-copy-value="true"
           data-copy-text-copied-message-value="Copied to your clipboard — paste it somewhere safe before you leave this page."
           data-copy-text-copy-failed-message-value="Your browser did not allow an automatic copy. Use Copy or Download below, before you leave this page."
           data-copy-text-download-filename-value="<%= revealed_token_filename(@revealed_token_name) %>">
        <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :warning, title: "This is the only time this token is shown") do %>
          <p>Only the SHA-256 digest is stored, so reloading this page will not show it again.</p>
          <% if @revealed_token_regenerated %>
            <p class="mt-1">
              The previous token for this key has stopped working — replace it anywhere it was in use.
            </p>
          <% end %>
          <%# Starts as the truth with JavaScript off, and is REPLACED by copy_text_controller with
              what actually happened. An auto-copy the browser blocked must not read as one that
              worked, so this never claims a copy the page has not yet made.

              `role="status"` because this line is the only report that the automatic copy was
              REFUSED, and it is swapped in after load — without it a screen reader is left holding
              the pre-JS sentence and never hears that the token is still uncopied. %>
          <p class="mt-1 font-semibold" role="status" data-copy-text-target="status">
            Copy it before you leave this page.
          </p>
        <% end %>

        <%# NO `data-controller="copy-text"` here, deliberately: this snippet is the AUTO-COPY
            scope's payload. Stimulus resolves a target to its nearest same-identifier ancestor
            (`Scope#containsElement` is `element.closest(controllerSelector) === this.element`), so
            wrapping this in its own scope would hide the token from the panel controller above —
            leaving its `sourceTarget` missing, and auto-copy and Download throwing on a panel whose
            entire job is to get this value off the page. The "Try it now" curl below takes the
            nested scope instead, for the same reason in reverse: `sourceTarget` is SINGULAR, so a
            second payload in THIS scope would be what auto-copy grabbed. %>
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">The key</p>
          <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new do %><%= @revealed_token %><% end %>
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
          <%= render UI::ButtonComponent.new(variant: :secondary, size: :sm, type: "button",
                                             data: { action: "copy-text#download" }) do %>
            Download as a file
          <% end %>
          <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
            Written from this page, not fetched from the server — SpecGuard does not hold the token.
          </p>
        </div>

        <%# == The integration, ready to paste, with the credential already in it
            ==================================================================================

            The whole point of what follows is that it is ready to paste: the real token is already
            in it, so there is no `<token>` to swap by hand while the value is still on screen. The
            persistent panel below keeps the by-name form, because it has to outlive the flash and
            by then the secret is unrecoverable.

            The same prompt the persistent "Wire this repository up" panel carries, from the same
            helper, differing in exactly one paragraph: here the real token is inlined, so what the
            reader pastes into a coding agent works as pasted. That is a property only THIS render
            can have. `ApiKey` stores a SHA-256 digest and the plaintext lives for this one
            flash-backed render, so every later render — including a plain reload of this page —
            can name the CI secret and nothing more.

            This REPLACES the "Next steps" list, which was correct and stopped short: store the key
            as a CI secret, point a client at `GET /api/v1/repository`, come back and check the
            stat. Every step of it was about the READ endpoint, which sends SpecGuard nothing, and
            a reader who followed it to the end had a working credential, a 200, and no telemetry.
            The write endpoint, the client gem, the linter and the annotation protocol are all on
            the guide the prompt points at, which is verified against the endpoint by
            `spec/requests/integration_guide_spec.rb`.

            Its OWN `copy-text` scope, so this longer payload cannot become the auto-copy scope's
            `sourceTarget` — see the note on "The key" above. Nested means its Copy button copies
            the prompt and the panel still auto-copies the bare token. %>
        <div class="space-y-1" data-controller="copy-text">
          <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Wire the repository up</p>
          <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= integration_guide_agent_prompt(
                repository: @repository,
                guide_url: integration_guide_url,
                endpoint: root_url.sub(%r{/+\z}, ""),
                token: @revealed_token
              ) %><% end %>
          <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
            Paste it into your coding agent. The key is already in it, so it works as pasted — which
            is true only while this page is on screen. Reading it yourself instead:
            <%= link_to "the integration guide", integration_guide_path, class: "underline" %>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <%# The auth check, kept and demoted. It was the whole of what this panel taught about
            using the key, under the heading "Try it now", directly above a list of next steps that
            never mentioned the write endpoint — so it read as the integration. It is not: it is one
            request that proves a credential authenticates and sends SpecGuard nothing. Retitled and
            labelled as exactly that, it is still the fastest way to tell a mistyped key from a
            misconfigured endpoint, which is worth keeping at the one moment the value is on screen.

            Its own `copy-text` scope, on the same rule as the block above. %>
        <div class="space-y-1" data-controller="copy-text">
          <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Or just check the key works</p>
          <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new do %>curl -H "Authorization: Bearer <%= @revealed_token %>" <%= api_v1_repository_url %><% end %>
          <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
            Ready to run as-is — the key above is already in the command, nothing to substitute. This
            is a connectivity check and not the integration: it reads, it sends nothing, and a
            <code>200</code> from it means the credential is good and nothing more.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
